begin;

select public.seed_cipmn_mock_module('CIPMN-MOD-011','[["DUCAP purpose and contextual delivery","A complex project uses a rigid imported approach that ignores local governance, community, cultural and sustainability realities.","Tailor a controlled and adaptive DUCAP approach that integrates local context, value, governance and delivery needs.","Apply the imported approach unchanged because global methods should never be contextualised.","Remove all controls so the project can respond quickly.","Focus only on technical completion and ignore post-delivery outcomes.","DUCAP combines controlled governance and adaptive delivery with explicit attention to Nigerian and project-specific context."],["Cultural, religious and political sensitivity","A site mobilisation plan conflicts with major community observances and local political protocols, creating resistance and access risk.","Engage relevant stakeholders, understand the context and adapt the plan without compromising lawful project objectives.","Proceed unchanged because cultural and political factors are outside project management.","Promise every stakeholder control over the project.","Address the issue only through security measures.","DUCAP requires informed sensitivity to cultural, religious and political realities while maintaining ethical and lawful governance."],["Adopt New Insights and lessons learned","A previous segment exposed a recurring design weakness, but the next segment begins with the same assumptions and process.","Capture, validate and apply the lesson before authorising the next segment.","Archive the lesson and continue because each segment should be independent.","Wait until the entire project closes before using lessons.","Apply the lesson informally without updating plans or controls.","DUCAP''s learning orientation requires new insights and lessons to influence current and future delivery, not merely be recorded."],["Project value justification","Costs and stakeholder impacts have increased significantly, while expected benefits have reduced, but the project continues without review.","Reassess project value justification and obtain a governance decision to continue, redirect, pause or stop.","Continue because initial approval remains valid regardless of change.","Judge value only by the percentage of budget spent.","Remove benefit measures from reports to avoid concern.","DUCAP requires continuing justification of project value, not unquestioned delivery of obsolete outputs."],["Segmented Delivery Management","A large project is managed as one uninterrupted block, so problems are discovered late and investment decisions cannot be staged.","Structure delivery into controlled segments with defined outcomes, reviews and authorisation points.","Create segments that have no clear outputs or decision criteria.","Use segments only as reporting labels while work remains uncontrolled.","Delay all review until final completion.","Segmented Delivery Management improves control, learning, value validation and decision-making through manageable delivery units."],["Financial sustainability","The project can be built with grant funding, but no credible plan exists for operating, maintaining or replacing the solution after handover.","Test whole-life affordability and define sustainable funding, ownership and maintenance arrangements before commitment.","Treat post-delivery cost as outside the project.","Assume future government budgets will automatically cover all expenses.","Reduce initial cost by removing maintenance planning.","DUCAP requires financial sustainability so benefits can continue after initial delivery funding ends."],["Team cohesion, wellbeing and equality","A high-pressure team excludes junior and female members from decisions, concentrates work on a few people and shows signs of burnout.","Restore inclusive participation, fair responsibility, wellbeing safeguards and cohesive team practices.","Keep the arrangement because speed is more important than equality and wellbeing.","Replace tired staff without addressing the work system.","Limit decisions to senior men to reduce discussion time.","DUCAP recognises team cohesion, wellbeing and equality as delivery enablers and ethical responsibilities."],["Understanding stakeholders","The project maps only formal authorities and misses informal community influencers who can enable or obstruct implementation.","Identify formal and informal stakeholders, analyse influence and needs, and plan continuous engagement.","Engage only stakeholders named in the contract.","Treat informal influence as illegitimate and irrelevant.","Wait until opposition emerges before mapping stakeholders.","DUCAP stakeholder understanding includes the wider social and institutional system affecting delivery and outcomes."],["Environmental and health impact","A proposed work method is faster but creates avoidable environmental damage and worker health exposure.","Assess the impacts and select a compliant method that protects environmental and health outcomes.","Use the faster method because schedule is the primary DUCAP objective.","Transfer responsibility to workers through a disclaimer.","Record the impact only after completion.","Environmental and health impacts are explicit DUCAP considerations and must be integrated into design and delivery."],["Post-delivery sustainability","A new facility is handed over without trained operators, spare-parts arrangements, ownership clarity or benefits monitoring.","Complete capability transfer, operational readiness, ownership and post-delivery sustainability arrangements.","Close the project once physical construction is complete.","Assume the beneficiary organisation will discover how to operate it.","Retain all knowledge with the contractor to encourage future dependence.","DUCAP extends attention beyond output completion to sustainable operation, outcomes and continued value."],["Scope and viability management","A proposed scope has attractive features but lacks evidence of feasibility, stakeholder demand, regulatory fit and benefit potential.","Evaluate scope and viability together before baselining and committing resources.","Approve the scope because stakeholders requested many features.","Assess viability only after procurement.","Use available budget as proof that the scope is viable.","DUCAP links scope decisions to viability, value, feasibility and contextual constraints."],["Resource management and RACI","Critical resources are overcommitted and several parties believe they are accountable for the same decision.","Develop a realistic resource plan and use a RACI or equivalent model to clarify responsibility and accountability.","Assign all roles to the project manager.","Allow multiple accountable owners for every decision.","Keep the schedule unchanged and expect teams to resolve conflicts informally.","DUCAP requires feasible resource arrangements and clear role interfaces to support controlled delivery."],["Quality, regulation and change management","A design change is introduced to solve a user problem but is not assessed against quality standards, regulation or other project controls.","Process the change through integrated assessment, including quality, regulatory, risk, schedule, cost and stakeholder effects.","Implement immediately because user value overrides regulation.","Reject every change because controlled projects cannot adapt.","Assess only the direct cost of the change.","DUCAP combines adaptability with controlled change and continued compliance across project dimensions."],["Risk, issue, schedule and outcome integration","Risk, schedule and outcome reports are prepared separately, so decision-makers cannot see how a threat affects milestones and expected benefits.","Integrate risk, issue, schedule and outcome information into a coherent decision view.","Keep reports separate to protect functional ownership.","Report only milestone dates because outcomes are measured later.","Remove uncertain benefits from governance discussions.","DUCAP emphasises integrated control so decisions consider delivery threats, timing and outcome realisation together."],["DUCAP process, SIPs and ethical vision","Segments hand work to one another informally, with unclear acceptance, unresolved risks and pressure to bypass ethical controls.","Use defined Segment Interaction Points with evidence, acceptance, unresolved-item visibility and adherence to the code of conduct.","Allow informal handover so segments can move faster.","Hide unresolved issues until the receiving segment encounters them.","Treat ethics as optional when delivery is urgent.","Segment Interaction Points support controlled transition, and DUCAP''s vision elements require ethical and transparent conduct throughout."]]'::jsonb);

commit;
